Durarara!! AU
Chapter 20 – Part 1
Curtis set the coffee aside, let his hand fall away from the guitar, and closed his eyes on the couch without fully meaning to commit to sleep. The flat was warm. The evening had finally gone quiet enough. Long black braids rested against the black compression shirt as his breathing slowed, the residual violence and unanswered complications of the day loosening their grip one fraction at a time. For a few minutes, solitude did exactly what he had asked it to do.
Then someone knocked.
Once.
Firm.
Not the hesitant knock of a neighbor.
Curtis did not rise. In the shallow edge of sleep the sound arrived like weather belonging to another room.
The second knock came harder.
On the other side of the door, Celty stood in the hallway with the yellow helmet under one arm and black smoke already rising in short, irritated bursts from the empty space above her collar. She had tried the phone. Then again. Then the line’s total failure had stopped looking like ordinary silence and started looking like a man removing himself from the board. The morning’s open door was still in her chest. The fight video was still in her head. The way he had thanked her and vanished afterward still sat wrong.
She knocked a third time, sharper.
Her real voice stayed sealed behind the door for now, but the private thought was already unkind.
If you drowned the phone and then climbed into a hole, Curtis, I swear—
She shifted her weight, boots silent on the hallway floor, and waited one more second for movement inside the flat. When none came, the irritation sharpened into something closer to genuine alarm constrained by pride. She had told him the offer to come back still stood. She had not expected to be using it the same night because the alternative was standing in a corridor outside a silent apartment, unable to tell whether he was only asleep…
or already past the point of wanting to be found.
The lock turned.
The door opened a few inches, then wider — and no one was standing there to do it.
Celty went still in the hallway, yellow helmet under one arm, black smoke rising in a sharp, immediate ribbon from the empty space above her collar as the apartment simply accepted her into it like a held breath being released. For a second she remained on the threshold, every courier instinct and every nonhuman sense checking for threat, trick, or wrongness. There was only the dim living room, the cooling air of an evening spent alone, and the quiet fact of a door that had opened without a visible hand.
She stepped in.
Curtis was on the couch.
Long black braids rested against the black compression shirt. One arm lay along the cushion. The guitar sat untouched beside him. He looked at her, mismatched eyes half-lidded and unguarded in a way that made the silence heavier than hostility, and said nothing. No yo. No sup. None of the small deflecting openings he used when he wanted the room to stay shallow. Just the look, and the fact that he had let her in without rising, without performance, without the usual first joke to keep the distance intact.
Celty shut the door behind her with one gloved hand.
Her real voice came through the private channel low and controlled, though the black smoke above her collar gave away the strain under it.
“…You didn’t answer the phone.”
A short pause. Her helmet remained under her arm as she took in the quiet flat, the coffee mug, the posture of a man who had clearly been trying to disappear without leaving the city.
“And then the door opens itself.” Another beat, tighter. “We’re going to talk about that. But first you’re going to tell me whether this silence is sleep, strategy, or you deciding the whole city can rot outside while you sit here turning attachment into a personal failure.”
She stepped farther into the room, not soft, not cruel — present.
“No deflection, Curtis. You already skipped the greeting. Don’t invent a new one now.”
Curtis remained on the couch, long black braids hanging forward against the black compression shirt, mismatched eyes dull in the dim flat. For a second it looked like he might reach for one of the old exits — a joke, a shrug, a practical excuse about sleep. Instead he only let the air out of his chest and answered with what was left.
“…I got nothing.”
The words were thin. Not dramatic. Just emptied.
Celty stood over the quiet room and let the black smoke rise harder from the empty space above her collar. The private channel stayed open, but she did not fill it immediately. I got nothing was not a puzzle to solve so much as a temperature reading, and the temperature was bad.
When her real voice came through, it was lower and steadier than the irritation that had brought her to the door.
“…Then don’t sit there like ‘nothing’ is a full sentence you’re required to finish alone.”
A short pause.
“You threw the whole day into violence, apology, retreat, and a dead line. I came anyway. The door opened anyway.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “If you truly have nothing left to perform, then fine. Sit with that. But I’m not leaving the room just because you ran out of armor. Silence is allowed. Disappearing through it is not.”
She moved closer to the couch, enough to make the presence concrete, not enough to force contact yet.
“You don’t have to entertain me. You don’t have to explain the fight in pretty order. You don’t even have to look glad I’m here.” Another quieter beat. “You just have to stop pretending ‘nothing’ means I should turn around and go back into the hallway.”
Curtis remained silent.
The confession was already sitting in the room whether he gave it language or not — the unlocked door, the empty hands, the deliberate quiet of a man who had removed every convenient point of contact and then sat down inside the consequence. Long black braids hung still against the black compression shirt. He did not look at the phone that was no longer there. He did not look away from the middle distance either.
Celty’s black smoke tightened above her collar.
“How come you’re not answering your phone?”
The question was plain. Practical on the surface. Underneath it lived every failed call from Anri, Rio, and her own line going nowhere.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes finally shifted toward her, dull and unguarded.
“…I threw it in the lake.”
For a second Celty did not move.
Then the black smoke burst upward in a sharp, incredulous ribbon, and her real voice came through the private channel low and lethal with restrained disbelief.
“…You threw it in the lake.”
Not a question this time. A restatement, as if hearing it twice might make the stupidity of the act more manageable.
“Of course you did. Of course the solution to being reached by people who care was to drown the device and walk home like that finished the problem.” She took one step closer to the couch, gloved hands flexing at her sides. “Do you have any idea how that reads from the outside? No ring. No message path. No way to tell whether you were sleeping, spiraling, or face-down in an alley after that fight video.”
Another harder beat.
“I told you the door was still open. That was not an invitation to erase every other means of contact and test whether anyone would physically come find the silence.” The empty line of her neck cut toward him. “You’re lucky I’m angry instead of only scared. And you’re going to sit there and let me be both.”
Curtis leaned farther into the couch, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as if even posture had become an effort. When he spoke again, the words came out practical first, the way he always tried to make damage sound manageable.
“I have access to my contacts and phone from my computer. If I get a new phone, it will be the same number. So my line is not totally dead.” A short pause. “I just need some time to think.”
The explanation sat in the dim flat like a temporary bridge over a worse truth. He did not say the rest out loud — that in times like this there had always been another body in the room, another voice cutting the spiral off before it became architecture, another presence named Justice who made the quiet less absolute. Without that, the lake and the locked door and the drowned line had felt like the only tools left.
Celty remained standing over him, black smoke rising in a slower but no less intense ribbon from the empty space above her collar. She did not soften immediately. She also did not miss the shape under the logistics.
“Time to think is not the same thing as cutting the whole city off and hoping the silence organizes itself into wisdom,” she said through the private channel. “A computer backup and a future replacement phone do not fix the part where people tried to reach you today and hit nothing. You didn’t buy distance. You manufactured a void.”
A shorter beat. Her voice lowered.
“And I can hear what you’re not putting in the sentence. You’re used to having someone beside you when it gets like this. Someone who kept the worst rooms from becoming the only rooms.” The smoke drifted toward him. “That absence is real. It does not entitle you to drown every remaining line and call the aftermath ‘thinking.’”
She took one more step into the living room’s quiet, helmet still under her arm.
“You can have time. I’m not here to strip that from you. But time with another presence in the flat is different from time spent convincing yourself that total isolation is the only structure left standing.” Another quieter line. “If what you need is room to think, take it. If what you need is not to do that thinking inside a grave you dug for every soft contact in your life… then stop treating a dead phone like a solution.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes finally lifted fully to the black smoke above her collar, the dullness in them hardening into something more exposed and defensive. Long black braids hung forward against the black compression shirt as his voice roughened.
“Did you come here to lecture me? Or was there a version you came to see?” A short, bitter pause. “Everyone seems to only want the nice parts… but forget those ugly parts are a part of who I am.”
The question cut cleanly through the room. Not only at Celty. At the whole day. At every pair of eyes that preferred the café listener, the rent-money foreigner, the careful protector — and flinched when the rest of him arrived with a cracked spine and no apology left for surviving the way he knew how.
Celty did not answer at once. Black smoke rose harder from the empty space above her collar, then steadied as if she were forcing herself not to take the easy defensive route. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was quieter than a lecture and far more dangerous in its honesty.
“…I came because the line went dead after a day that already had blood and retreat in it.”
A short pause.
“If all I wanted was the nice parts, I would have stayed on the road and told myself the fight video was just another city spectacle. I wouldn’t be standing in your flat after you drowned your phone.” The smoke drifted toward him. “I have seen more than your gentle hours. I saw the extraction. I saw the way you talk about the people who prey on the weak. I saw what the old rooms do to your eyes when they open without permission. None of that was the polished version.”
Another heavier beat.
“So no. I did not come for a convenient Curtis. I came for the one who goes silent when the ugly parts get stared at too hard. The one who decides, too quickly, that if the whole of him cannot be held then no one should be able to reach him at all.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “If you think I’m only here to sand those edges down into something prettier, you’re wrong. But if you use the ugly parts as a reason to deny every remaining form of contact, then you’re not protecting your whole self. You’re orphaning it.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“I can stand the parts of you that are not nice. What I can’t stand is watching you drown the means of being known the moment those parts become visible.”
Curtis lifted the mug, discovered only cold coffee, and drank it down anyway. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he lowered the empty cup with a flat little nod.
“…Delicious.”
Then he reached for the guitar beside the couch and lightly strummed once across the strings — not a song, not even an intentional progression, just a thin thread of sound drawn through the tense air like he needed proof that ordinary objects still worked in a room this heavy. The note hung briefly and faded.
Celty stared at him.
Black smoke rose in a short, incredulous ribbon from the empty space above her collar. For a second the private channel held only the static of someone watching a man answer a serious confrontation with cold caffeine and a single guitar stroke.
When her real voice came through, it was dry enough to cut paper.
“…Really.”
A short pause.
“I tell you I can stand the ugly parts, and your response is expired coffee theater and one strum of emotional misdirection.” The smoke drifted toward the guitar. “If that is supposed to convince me you’re fine, it is doing the opposite. If it is supposed to change the subject, it is also doing the opposite.”
Another beat, lower, less sharp than reluctant.
“You can drink cold coffee. You can touch the guitar. I’m not confiscating your coping props.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “But if the next sound out of you is only more performance emptiness, I’m taking the instrument out of your hands and putting it back against the wall until you finish the actual sentence you started when you said you had nothing.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“Play if you need the motion. Just don’t use it as a door out of the room I’m still standing in.”
Curtis set the empty mug aside and stood.
Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he hooked the guitar by the neck and turned toward the hall. The motion was quieter than a retreat, more deliberate than a dodge, but it still emptied the couch she had been trying to keep him on.
“I’m not running.” His voice stayed even. “I’m going to the roof for a cigarette.”
Celty knew the rule of the flat at once. No smoking indoors. He had said it before without ceremony, a hard little boundary in a place that otherwise absorbed chaos with surprising tolerance. The irony of it sat sharply in her awareness for half a second — a headless woman whose shadow-smoke could fill a room, and a man whose own hidden white smoke had already opened a door without his hands, both of them constrained by the ordinary etiquette of not fouling the apartment air. No one in Ikebukuro except the dead brother who had shared that power would have caught the second half of the joke. Curtis still carried it alone.
She watched him take the guitar with him.
Black smoke rose in a tighter ribbon from the empty space above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel low and unimpressed, though not purely accusatory.
“…Of course you are.”
A short pause.
“Not running. Just relocating the conversation to open air again with nicotine and a stringed instrument, under the one household rule that makes the exit look reasonable.” The smoke drifted after him toward the hall. “Fine. Roof. Cigarette. I know the boundary.”
Another beat, firmer.
“But if the guitar is coming, then so am I. You don’t get to reframe this as a smoke break and disappear into the skyline with the only object left that still gives your hands something honest to do.”
She adjusted the yellow helmet under her arm and started after him.
“Lead the way, Curtis. If we’re doing the roof version again, we’re doing it without the part where you pretend the altitude makes you unreachable.”
The roof was cooler than the flat, evening air moving clean across the ledge.
Curtis sat down like a man returning to a familiar chair, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he settled with one knee up and the guitar across his lap. Celty remained a few steps back at first, black smoke rising in cautious ribbons from the empty space above her collar, yellow helmet still under her arm. She had followed him for the cigarette and the unfinished conversation. She had not accounted for the rest.
She only noticed the bottle when he lifted it.
Clear glass. Cheap vodka. No ceremony. Curtis had brought it up with the same quiet efficiency he used for everything he did not want argued in advance. He took a drink, grimaced faintly at the burn, then smiled — gentle, almost polite, as if the ledge and the liquor and the city spread beneath them were all part of some private joke he was finally willing to share out loud.
Celty’s smoke spiked hard.
“…Curtis.”
He did not answer with words.
His fingers found the guitar instead and laid down a tune that had no business belonging to the hour — light, almost bright, the kind of progression that could have sold summer if the voice over it had cooperated. Then he began to sing, and the contradiction became unbearable.
His singing voice was amazing.
Clear. Controlled. Beautiful enough to make the first lines feel like a trap. The melody stayed almost buoyant while the words walked straight off the edge with him:
My legs are dangling off the edge
The bottom of the bottle is my only friend…
Celty went rigid.
Black smoke poured upward from the empty space above her collar as the private channel filled with the wrongness of it — the gentle smile, the pretty guitar line, the vodka on the ledge, and a voice too skilled to hide inside what it was actually saying. Gone, gone, gone. Pills. Bullet. Twenty stories up. The letter. The sirens. The sharpened knife. The suit for meeting God.
It was not a performance for an audience.
It was a man using a lovely instrument to tell the exact truth he would not give her in straight sentences on the couch.
Curtis kept playing, kept singing, eyes half-lidded toward the skyline as if the city might answer the chorus back. The happy shape of the tune made every dark line land harder. On the roof, with the bottle open and the drop beneath his dangling stillness, the song stopped being content from another life and became a present-tense danger wearing a beautiful voice.
Celty’s real voice cut into the private channel, low and locked.
“Curtis. Stop.”
He smiled gently through the next line anyway, long black braids moving in the wind, and for one awful second it was impossible to tell whether he was confessing, coping, or testing how far the ledge could listen before someone on the roof with him refused to treat the song like only music.
Celty did not move until the last soft residue of the tune left the strings.
Black smoke poured from the empty space above her collar in thick, uncontrolled ribbons, torn sideways by the roof wind. The private channel opened on a voice stripped of every earlier irritation and replaced with something colder and more frightened under the control.
“…That is not a song you play as coping furniture.”
A short, hard pause.
“A bright little melody over those words is not charm, Curtis. It is a warning dressed up as entertainment. You brought a bottle to the ledge, smiled at me, and then used a beautiful voice to walk through every version of leaving a body can invent.” The smoke dragged toward him. “Do not sit there and treat that like atmosphere. Not in front of me. Not after drowning your phone and telling me you had nothing left.”
Curtis let out a deep sigh.
The guitar settled quieter against his lap as he tipped his head back and stared at the moon, long black braids falling away from his face. For a second the city beneath them might as well have been another country.
“…Justice left me that song after he died.” A thin, rough breath. “It tells both our stories.” He huffed a soft, humorless little laugh. “Heheh. You were just as cruel as me at times.”
The name landed on the roof like a third presence.
Celty’s smoke stuttered, then rose harder. When her real voice came back, it was lower — less attack, more careful gravity around a grief that had just put its hands on the present tense.
“…He left you that.”
Not quite a question. A placement of horror and understanding at once.
“So every time you sing it, you’re not only performing despair. You’re keeping a dead man’s final cruelty and final honesty in your mouth at the same time.” Another beat, quieter. “No wonder it sounds like both of you. No wonder you can smile while you do it. That isn’t lightness. That’s what shared damage looks like when one of the pair is gone and the other one still has the lyrics.”
She stepped closer to the ledge, not close enough to startle, close enough to make the drop stop being private.
“Listen to me. You can tell me it is his song. You can tell me it is your story. What you cannot do is sit on a roof with a bottle and that chorus and expect me to hear only memorial.” The empty line of her neck cut toward him. “I hear a man who is still deciding whether surviving the list, the city, and the people attaching themselves to him is endurance… or just a longer verse before the last gone.”
Black smoke curled tight in the wind between them.
“Put the bottle down, Curtis. If this is grief, I will sit in it with you. If it is something closer to a plan, I am not leaving you alone with the moon and his song.”
The song did not get to finish becoming an argument.
A scream split the parking lot below — sharp, female, terrified. Celty’s headless posture snapped toward the edge at once. Curtis was already moving. Down in the half-lit asphalt expanse, a woman clutched at a bleeding line along her arm while an old man lay crumpled near a fallen shopping bag. A masked figure sprinted for the alley with a purse banging against his side.
Curtis’s face changed.
“…You son of a bitch.”
The guitar went aside. The vodka bottle stayed in his hand as he stepped off the roof without hesitation.
He hit the parking lot in a superhero landing hard enough to crack the surface under one knee and open palm, long black braids whipping forward against the black compression shirt. Then he was sprinting, pure explosive speed across the open ground. Mid-run he tipped the bottle back, drained the last of the vodka, and without breaking stride hurled the empty glass with sheer brutal force.
The bottle crossed the dark like a thrown judgment and exploded against the masked man’s head.
Glass, blood, and momentum went in different directions. The robber staggered, dropped, and barely understood the ground before Curtis reached him, seized one ankle, and dragged him back into the light with the stolen purse recovered in the other hand. The efficiency of it was ugly and complete.
He walked toward the couple at a controlled pace.
The purse went back first. Then Curtis dropped to a knee beside the old man, mismatched eyes scanning for worse damage than pride and pavement.
“Hey, mister? You okay?”
The old man pushed up with his wife’s shaking help, breath rough, eyes wide at the tall foreigner who had fallen out of the sky and ended the robbery in one throw.
“He—he tried to rob us. Punched me when I grabbed for the bag. My wife—”
“I can wait here until the cops arrive,” Curtis said.
The old man shook his head hard, already thinking in the practical panic of people who did not want a scene to become more complicated than survival.
“No, no. Go. We’ll explain that someone helped us. Thank you so much.”
Curtis looked at the unconscious masked man, then at the couple, then at the blood and broken glass.
“….”
He nodded once.
Then he began walking back toward the building, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt, residual violence still in his hands and the taste of cheap vodka still in his mouth — leaving the grateful couple, the downed robber, and the cracked parking lot behind him as if leaping off a roof to end a mugging were simply the next item after a dead man’s song.
High above, Celty remained at the ledge for one stunned second longer before black smoke surged and she moved to follow.
Curtis made it back under the building’s shadow before the residual heat of the chase fully left his hands. Then he saw the parking surface properly.
A spiderweb of cracked asphalt marked the exact point of his landing — deep, obvious, and absolutely not something a normal tenant produced on an evening walk.
He stopped dead, long black braids swinging forward against the black compression shirt.
“…Aw, fuck!!! My landlord’s going to kill me. Shit!!! The ground!!!”
From above, Celty’s real voice snapped down through the private channel at once, black smoke already trailing as she descended the roof access hard enough to make the irritation audible.
“That is what you’re worried about right now?!”
A short, incredulous pause.
“You were on a ledge with a bottle and a dead man’s suicide song. Then you jumped off a building, glassed a robber mid-sprint, and casually walked back like the parking lot was a side quest — and the first full sentence out of you is landlord liability?” The smoke dragged after her as she hit the lower level and came into view. “Curtis. Look at me. Are you hurt. Is the couple alive. Is the man you bottled still breathing. Then, and only then, do we discuss the structural damage your landing did to the asphalt.”
She stopped a few paces from him, yellow helmet under one arm, the empty line of her neck cutting toward the cracked ground and then back to his face.
“Also, yes. That looks expensive. No, that is not the primary crisis of the last ten minutes.”
By the time they got back into the suite, the parking-lot panic had thinned into residual adrenaline and the quieter fact of two people re-entering a room that had already held too much evening.
Curtis shut the door, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt, and crossed into the living space without the stumble, glaze, or loosened mouth that that much vodka should have bought him. His eyes were tired. His posture was heavy. But the drunken collapse Celty had every reason to expect simply was not there. He looked surprisingly sober — frustratingly so — as if the bottle on the roof and the last hard swallow mid-sprint had been processed by a body that refused to give her even that ordinary human consequence.
Celty stopped just inside the flat and stared at him.
Black smoke rose in a slow, unsettled ribbon from the empty space above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel low and sharply attentive.
“…You’re upright.”
A short pause.
“You drank enough on that roof to put most people on the floor, then finished the bottle on the run, and now you’re standing in your living room talking about landlord fees like the alcohol was a mild suggestion.” The smoke drifted toward him. “That is not normal recovery, Curtis. Not after a song like that. Not after a jump like that. Not after the way the last hour has gone.”
Another tighter beat as she set the yellow helmet down on the table hard enough to make the point.
“I’m glad you’re not slurring into a blackout. Don’t mistake that for me being comforted. A man who can throw back that much vodka and still look this composed is either far more practiced at functioning through poison than he should be… or there is something in him that processes damage differently from ordinary flesh.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“Which one am I looking at right now?”
Curtis shrugged once, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he crossed to the counter like the evening could still be reduced to logistics.
“I can handle my liquor pretty well. Plus my blood was pumping so much, I’m pretty much half and half right now.”
He said it casually, almost dismissively — the trained survivor’s habit of offering the least extraordinary explanation that still fit the facts.
Celty did not look convinced.
Black smoke rose in a thin, skeptical ribbon from the empty space above her collar as her real voice came through the private channel, dry and unyielding.
“…Half and half.”
A short pause.
“You were on a ledge singing a dead man’s suicide song with a bottle in hand, then you dropped off a building, sprinted across a parking lot, and still had the coordination to put a glass bottle through a robber’s head at range. Now you’re home, upright, articulate, and giving me blood was pumping like that accounts for the whole picture.” The smoke drifted toward him. “I’m not saying adrenaline is fake. I’m saying your version of ‘half and half’ looks a lot like most people’s completely sober, and that pattern keeps showing up around you whenever ordinary limits should be visible.”
Another firmer beat.
“Fine. Handle your liquor. Ride the adrenaline. Use whatever explanation lets you keep the evening from becoming a medical conversation.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “But don’t expect me to file this under normal human tolerance and move on. Too many things about you only make sense if the baseline is already wrong.”
The empty line of her neck cut toward him.
“Half and half or not, you’re still not off the hook for the song, the lake phone, or the fact that I had to come find you after the line went dead. Sit down before the adrenaline story runs out and whatever is underneath it has to stand on its own.”
Curtis did not sit down.
He crossed to the closet instead, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he pushed past ordinary storage and came back with something decidedly nicer than the rooftop vodka — dark glass, better label, the kind of bottle that belonged to celebration or deliberate self-sabotage depending on the hour. He smiled at it with thin satisfaction.
“Ahh. This will do.”
He poured a glass, neat and unhurried, then turned back toward Celty with the same mild expression he used when he wanted a dangerous conversation to sound like hospitality.
“So. Aside from me throwing my phone in the lake… what brings you here tonight?”
Celty stared at him hard enough that the black smoke above her collar spiked in a single violent ribbon.
Her real voice came through the private channel flat and incredulous.
“…Put the glass down.”
A short pause. When he didn’t immediately obey, the smoke only thickened.
“What brings me here is the fight video, the dead line, the fact that three different attempts to reach you went nowhere, and the version of you that answered one call with gratitude and then erased the phone entirely. What brings me here is a roof, a bottle, and a song Justice left you that walks through every method a person can use to stop remaining in the world. What brings me here is you jumping off a building afterward like the emotional crisis had simply changed costume into civic duty.”
She stepped farther into the room, yellow helmet still on the table where she’d left it, the empty line of her neck fixed on him.
“So no. You do not get to pour nicer liquor and interview me like I’m an unexpected guest at a dinner party. I have questions, and you’re going to hear them.”
Another harder beat.
“First: after that song, were you grieving, testing me, or actively thinking about following it off the ledge.
Second: why is total isolation always your first structural solution when people see the ugly parts.
Third: how many more ordinary explanations am I supposed to accept before you admit that your body and your recovery do not behave like the story you’re selling.
And fourth—” the smoke dragged toward the glass in his hand, “—why are you still drinking when the last bottle already helped turn this night into a crisis response.”
She held the silence after the list, unyielding.
“Pick one to answer first. Preferrably before you finish that glass.”
Curtis turned the glass once in his hand, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as a dry edge entered his voice.
“Did you drill Shizuo this hard? He’s pretty much like I am.”
Celty’s black smoke burst upward from the empty space above her collar in pure disbelief.
“No,” her real voice snapped through the private channel. “He is not.”
A short, sharp pause.
“Shizuo is a walking disaster with a short fuse and a city-shaped collateral range. He breaks furniture, throws vending machines, and solves interpersonal conflict by turning the street into a physics problem. That is not the same thing as you.” The smoke dragged toward Curtis. “You disappear. You drown phones. You sit on ledges with dead men’s songs and then jump off buildings to bottle robbers like the crisis can be converted into usefulness mid-fall. You talk about lists ending. You talk about aging out of everyone you attach to. You thank me for calling and then erase the line.”
Another harder beat.
“I do not ‘drill’ Shizuo like this because Shizuo does not invite me into the private architecture of his grief and then ask me to accept total isolation as a coping strategy. He also does not sit on my emotional threshold overnight, open a door on purpose, and then act surprised when I show up after the silence turns dangerous.” Her gloved hand cut once through the air. “You are not the same. Stop hiding behind him like shared capacity for violence makes the interior identical.”
The empty line of her neck fixed on him.
“Shizuo is a public hazard. You are a private collapse with excellent aim. Different problem. Different response. Now answer one of the actual questions.”
Curtis drank from the nicer glass, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as the answers finally came out in order, flat and unadorned.
“First. Falling off that ledge won’t kill me, like how it wouldn’t kill Shizuo or Justice. I’m upset, grieving, and I’m confused about a lot of shit.”
A short pause. His mismatched eyes stayed on the amber surface of the drink.
“Second. Isolation was never my go-to. If things got heavy, someone would be home. Now no one is. I don’t have someone like Shinra to come home to. Last two people who got that close… one blew his fuckin’ brains out. And the stupid bitch I trusted kicked my heart in the ass.”
Another beat, rougher.
“Third. I grew up in Appalachia. Everything about my body is not ordinary. You’ve seen my scars.”
He tipped the glass slightly, almost in salute to the fourth point.
“Fourth. I’m drinking because I’m an adult who can do what he wants. I was going to drink regardless if you showed up or not.”
The suite held the full set of answers like a room that had been waiting for them and now regretted it.
Celty did not speak at once. Black smoke rose in a dense, controlled column from the empty space above her collar, the private channel open and dangerously quiet while she sorted the pieces: a man who believed the drop would not kill him, a grief still organized around a brother who had chosen a gun, an ex who had finished the lesson that closeness ends in damage, a non-ordinary body she had already seen mapped in scars, and a present-tense refusal to let her presence rewrite the bottle in his hand.
When her real voice came through, it was lower than anger and far more exacting.
“…Thank you for finally answering.”
A short pause.
“Do not mistake that for me accepting every piece as safe. You saying the ledge would not kill you does not make the song, the bottle, or the jump feel smaller. It makes them more specific. You’re not only reckless. You’re operating on a private physics where ordinary endings do not apply, and that makes grief more dangerous, not less — because you can survive falls that would stop other people and still not survive the part that keeps sending you up there with a dead man’s lyrics.”
The smoke drifted toward him.
“I heard the second answer. Someone used to be home. Now the rooms are empty, and the last two times you let people close, one died by his own hand and one made loyalty feel like a humiliation. That explains the drowned phone. It does not make total isolation righteous. It makes it familiar.” A thinner beat. “And leave Shinra out of this as a comparison for what I am doing in your doorway. I did not come here to be your replacement soft landing. I came because the line went dead after the day split open.”
Another firmer line.
“The Appalachia answer I already half-knew. Scars. Recovery. Impact that should have dropped you and didn’t. We’re not finished with that, but I’m not pretending ordinary is still on the table.”
Her gloved hand cut once toward the glass.
“As for the fourth… fine. You’re an adult. Drink. I’m not your keeper and I’m not impressed by the performance of autonomy either. What I care about is whether the bottle is company or fuel. After the song I heard on that roof, you don’t get to pretend those are the same thing.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, unyielding and not leaving.
“You answered. Good. Now stay in the room with the answers instead of washing them down until they look smaller than they are.”
Curtis’s hand tightened around the glass until the line of his knuckles stood out against the black compression shirt. Long black braids hung forward as his mismatched eyes finally went flat and final.
“Get out.”
No joke under it. No soft landing. Just the door slamming shut in two words after he had already given her more of the ugly architecture than he usually let anyone walk through alive.
Celty stopped where she stood.
Black smoke burst upward from the empty space above her collar, then locked into a hard, controlled column as the private channel filled with the impact of the dismissal. For a second she only faced him in the suite’s dim light, yellow helmet still on the table behind her, the whole night’s sequence — dead phone, roof song, jump, bottle, answers — ending in an eviction.
When her real voice came through, it was quieter than the anger and more dangerous for it.
“…Say that again carefully.”
A short pause.
“You opened the door without touching it. You let me in. You answered the questions. And now that the answers are sitting in the room with us, your solution is to throw me back into the hallway?” The smoke dragged toward him. “No. Not like that. Not after the ledge and the song and the part where you admitted the drop wouldn’t kill you.”
Another firmer beat.
“If you want me to leave because you need the flat empty to breathe, say that. If you’re ordering me out because I got too close to the real shape of you, then at least own the cowardice in the distinction.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “I am not furniture you can drag in for crisis response and put back outside the moment the honesty becomes inconvenient.”
The empty line of her neck remained fixed on him.
“So which is it, Curtis. Space… or retreat dressed up as a command?”
Curtis did not take the distinction.
He did not redefine it as space. He did not soften it into a request. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as his mismatched eyes stayed locked on the empty place above her collar, the glass still hard in his hand.
“Get out now.”
The second version landed heavier than the first. Not louder, exactly — final. The room seemed to shrink around the command, every earlier opening of the night collapsing into a single hard point: whatever door he had left unlocked that morning was closed again, and she was on the wrong side of it for his tolerance.
Celty stood there for one full second in the shock of being truly ejected.
Black smoke tore upward from the empty space above her collar in a violent ribbon, then cut short as if even the shadow had been slapped back. The private channel opened on a voice stripped of negotiation and left with something colder, wounded under the control.
“…Fine.”
A short, sharp pause.
“You want the room empty? You have it.” She turned, crossed to the table, and took the yellow helmet in one gloved hand hard enough that the motion sounded like a verdict. “You can sit here with your nicer bottle and your non-ordinary body and the ghost of every person who got close enough to ruin the place for company. That is your choice.”
At the door she stopped just long enough to leave the last line behind her without looking back.
“But don’t you dare thank me for calling next time if the only thing you do with concern is drown it, drink over it, and order it out of the flat when it survives the trip to your door.”
The door opened.
The door shut.
Celty’s boots hit the hallway hard, black smoke seething under the helmet as she sealed it on, and for the first time since the morning’s invitation, the distance between them was not complicated.
It was enforced.
The suite did not hold him long after the door closed on Celty.
Curtis set the glass down hard enough to make the liquor jump, crossed into the bedroom, and pulled the night gear from the closet with the same blunt efficiency he used for exits that were no longer negotiable. Gray hoodie over the black compression shirt. Black Jordan cap tugged low. Sunglasses. Mask sealing the lower half of his face until the man in the mirror became only a silhouette with long braids tucked away and no expression left to read. By the time he hit the hallway, Curtis Nightstar had been reduced to a moving absence — tall, anonymous, already pointed at the street.
He took the stairs instead of the elevator.
He did not check the corridor for a yellow helmet.
He did not look back.
Outside, the night air hit cooler and cleaner than the flat’s spoiled quiet. Curtis shoved his hands into the hoodie pocket and started walking with no destination more sophisticated than motion, long strides eating the sidewalk as if distance itself might finish the argument the room had not.
What he did not know was that Celty had not truly left.
She had put the helmet on in the outer stairwell and waited in the shadow of the building’s flank long enough to see the masked figure emerge. Black smoke sealed tight under the yellow shell as Shooter stayed dark and silent where she had left it. For a second the private channel filled with pure conflicted impulse — anger at the eviction, fear from the roof song, the hard memory of get out now still ringing against the morning’s open door.
Then she started following on foot.
Not close enough for the average person to notice.
Close enough that the Black Rider’s attention never fully released the tall gray hoodie cutting through Ikebukuro’s night.
Curtis moved like a man who believed he had successfully emptied the board.
Behind him, Celty moved like a woman who had decided that being ordered out of the flat was not the same thing as surrendering the night.
The walk had no clean destination until the tower appeared and made one.
Curtis cut through side streets and service access with the unthinking precision of someone who had been using height as a coping structure for years. Gray hoodie. Black Jordan cap. Mask and sunglasses sealing the face he no longer wanted read. Behind him, far enough to remain a suspicion rather than a presence, Celty kept pace through the city’s blind angles, yellow helmet angled up each time the tall figure chose another vertical path.
The tower was very high.
By the time Curtis reached the upper ledge, Ikebukuro had become a circuit board of light far below, wind harder and cleaner than anything the street could offer. He swung one leg out over the drop, then the other, and sat with the night under his heels like a held breath. For a moment he only stayed there, gloved by anonymity, a masked outline against the dark.
Then he pulled the hoodie down.
The gray fabric fell back from his head and shoulders, and the long black braids came free into the wind — two dark ropes against the black cap and mask, the same silhouette the street had already begun turning into rumor. Sunglasses still hid his eyes. The mask still sealed his mouth. But the disguise had loosened into something more honest by one degree: not the fully covered ghost of the sidewalk, not the bare-faced man from the suite. Something in between. A figure on a ledge with his braids out and the city under him, looking less like a man who had successfully left the night behind and more like one who had only climbed high enough to stop being followed by ordinary means.
He did not know Celty was still below the last rise of access, black smoke tight under the helmet, staring up at the exact moment the hoodie came down.
From her angle, it was unmistakable.
Not a stranger.
Not a rumor.
Curtis — on another ledge, in another mask, with the drop under him again and no phone left in the world to make the reach easy.
